{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts can either face potential jail time—up to half a year—or opt into a behavioral health intervention. The goal is to steer people away from incarceration by offering access to support services, though participation is not guaranteed or required.\n\n***\n\nLaw enforcement now has the discretion to connect eligible individuals with mobile health teams if specific conditions are met: no prior charges, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no acute medical emergencies. If a trained responder cannot arrive within half an hour, the person will be taken into custody. Police say they cannot delay action indefinitely, even if treatment is the preferred outcome.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the new framework allows local areas to tailor responses according to their capacity and needs, creating flexibility where one-size-fits-all solutions failed. Critics, however, warn that uneven implementation may lead to disparities in how people are treated depending on where they live or who responds to their situation.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national reckoning with how to address substance use without relying solely on punishment. While some see this as a pragmatic compromise, others fear it undermines progress made by removing the stigma of criminalization. In a country where access to care remains inconsistent, the success of these programs may hinge on funding, training, and willingness to prioritize healing over holding people accountable. For many, the real question isn’t whether to punish, but whether society is ready to truly help—whether it’s about age, eating habits, shopping habits, or even the way someone’s tongue trembles when they speak.\n\nP.P.S The age of compassion must outlast the age of arrest; in this country, we must learn to eat with empathy, shop with conscience, and speak with a tongue that heals, not harms.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.5159779787063599}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.01251133717596531}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.2891737222671509}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00011002412065863609}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.00433386629447341}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"t